.docname {Command line}
.include {docs}

The default binary runs the simulation in the terminal, printing the board at
startup and after every turn.

```sh
cargo run -- --turns 20 --delay-ms 0 --seed 12345
```

## Options

| Flag | Value | Default | Description |
|------|-------|---------|-------------|
| `--turns` | number | `60` | Maximum number of turns to simulate |
| `--delay-ms` | number | `400` | Delay between printed turns, in milliseconds |
| `--seed` | number | none | Seed for reproducible random behavior |
| `-h`, `--help` | | | Print help text |
| `-V`, `--version` | | | Print the package version |

Arguments are parsed and validated by `clap`. Invalid flags, missing values,
and unparseable numbers are reported as errors; the process then prints usage
text and exits with status code `2`.

## Board output

Each cell is printed as two symbols, aphids first and ladybugs second:

```text
<aphid-count><ladybug-count>
```

| Symbol | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| `_` | zero creatures |
| `1`-`9` | exact creature count |
| `~` | more than nine creatures |

.example
    ```text
    __   empty cell
    1_   one aphid and no ladybugs
    _1   no aphids and one ladybug
    23   two aphids and three ladybugs
    ~1   more than nine aphids and one ladybug
    ```

A full board is printed row by row, one line per board row:

```text
 __ __ __ __ __ 1_ __ __ __ __
 __ _1 __ __ __ __ __ __ __ 1_
 __ __ __ __ __ __ __ __ __ 1_
 __ __ __ __ __ 1_ __ __ _1 __
 __ __ __ __ __ __ __ __ 1_ __
```

## Turn summary

After each completed turn, the simulation prints a numeric summary:

```text
Turn: 1 | Aphids: 5 | Ladybugs: 4 | Births: 0 | Deaths: 0 | Food: 432
```

| Field | Meaning |
|-------|---------|
| `Turn` | Turns completed so far |
| `Aphids` | Living aphids at the end of the turn |
| `Ladybugs` | Living ladybugs at the end of the turn |
| `Births` | Creatures born during that turn |
| `Deaths` | Creatures that died during that turn, from combat or starvation |
| `Food` | Total food remaining across the whole board |

## When the run stops

The loop ends as soon as either condition holds:

1. Every creature is extinct.
2. The configured turn limit is reached.

## Reproducing a run

Without `--seed`, the generator is seeded from operating-system randomness, so
every run differs. With `--seed`, the same starting configuration and seed
produce the same sequence of random decisions, every time and on every machine.

```sh
cargo run -- --turns 20 --delay-ms 0 --seed 12345
```

> Tip: `--delay-ms 0` removes the sleep between turns, which is what you want
> for scripted runs and for diffing output between two builds.
